%I A400417 #18 Nov 11 2026 16:28:49
%S A400417 2,8,16,28,42,64
%N A400417 Diameter of the directed Cayley graph of the alternating group A_(2n+1) generated by L = (0,1,...,2n) and T = (0,1,2).
%C A400417 Equivalently, a(n) is the smallest integer d such that every element of the alternating group A_{2n+1} can be expressed as a product of at most d factors, each equal to L or T, where inverses are not included as additional generators.
%C A400417 In one-line notation, right multiplication by L shifts the entries cyclically one position to the left, and right multiplication by T replaces the first three entries [x, y, z] by [y, z, x].
%C A400417 The terms were computed by an exhaustive breadth-first search from the identity permutation. Since this is a Cayley graph, its diameter equals the maximum distance from the identity.
%e A400417 For n=1, both generators equal (0,1,2). The directed Cayley graph of A_3 is a directed cycle on three vertices, so a(1)=2.
%e A400417 For n=2, exhaustive breadth-first search visits all 60 elements of A_5, and the maximum distance from the identity is 8, so a(2)=8.
%o A400417 (Python)
%o A400417 from collections import deque
%o A400417 def a(n):
%o A400417     m = 2*n + 1
%o A400417     start = tuple(range(m))
%o A400417     dist = {start: 0}
%o A400417     queue = deque([start])
%o A400417     while queue:
%o A400417         p = queue.popleft()
%o A400417         neighbors = (
%o A400417             p[1:] + p[:1],
%o A400417             p[1:3] + p[:1] + p[3:]
%o A400417         )
%o A400417         for q in neighbors:
%o A400417             if q not in dist:
%o A400417                 dist[q] = dist[p] + 1
%o A400417                 queue.append(q)
%o A400417     return max(dist.values())
%o A400417 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 5)])
%Y A400417 Cf. A039745 (directed diameter for an n-cycle and a transposition), A186783 (the corresponding undirected diameter).
%K A400417 nonn,more,hard,new
%O A400417 1,1
%A A400417 _Sergei Uskov_, Nov 05 2026
